# ============================================================================
# EVAL SHEET: "Brightness 1/2" — side-lit sphere AFTER translation
# ============================================================================
# Requirement (verbatim): "Place a sphere at some coordinates resulting from
# a translation, the camera facing the sphere, and place a spot left or
# right of the camera but positioned in such a way that the sphere will be
# lit sideways. Display the rendered image. The sphere should be visible,
# properly illuminated and displayed without glitching. Properly means that
# the halo of light should be computed after translation, not before."
#
# The bug this test is designed to catch: computing the diffuse/specular
# highlight using the sphere's pre-translation centre (e.g. {0,0,0}) instead
# of its actual (translated) centre. Here the sphere is moved well off the
# world origin — to {6,2,5} — so if lighting were mistakenly computed
# against {0,0,0} the highlight would land on the wrong side of the visible
# sphere entirely, which is easy to spot.

#ambient strength/ratio RGB_col( , , )
A 0.2 255,255,255

#camera pos( , , ) camera_dir( , , ) field_of_view(in deg)
# Camera is re-aimed to keep facing the sphere at its new location: same x/y
# as the sphere (6,2), pulled back on z.
C 6,2,-10 0,0,1 70

#L pos( , , ) brightness_ratio RGB_col( , , )
# Light sits to the left of the camera/sphere (x=0 vs. sphere's x=6) and
# near the sphere's own depth (z=3, close to the sphere's z=5), so it lights
# the translated sphere from the side — proving the halo tracks the
# sphere's real (post-translation) position.
L 0,2,3 0.8 255,255,255

#sp sphere_center( , , ) diameter RGB_colour( , , )
# Sphere centre {6,2,5} — the "coordinates resulting from a translation"
# the eval sheet asks for.
sp 6,2,5 4 255,0,0
